---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BlogTemplate from "../../components/BlogTemplate.astro";

// IMAGES (reuse or replace later)
import hero from "../../assets/images/market.jpg";
import auto from "../../assets/images/3.png";
import shop from "../../assets/images/mrp.webp";
import upi from "../../assets/images/upi.jpg";
import CTA from "../../components/CTA.astro";
import FAQ from "../../components/FAQ.astro";

const faqs = [
    {
        question: "Do tourists always get overcharged in Delhi?",
        answer: "Not necessarily. Most overcharging happens due to lack of price awareness. Using apps and understanding basic rules reduces this significantly.",
    },
    {
        question: "Is bargaining necessary everywhere?",
        answer: "No. Bargaining is common in local markets but not required for low-value items or fixed-price shops.",
    },
    {
        question: "Is it safe to refuse extra charges?",
        answer: "Yes. As long as you remain calm and clear, refusing unagreed charges is completely acceptable.",
    },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="How to Avoid Getting Overcharged in Delhi | Delhi Concierge">
    <BlogTemplate
        title="How to Avoid Getting Overcharged in Delhi"
        heroImage={hero}
        breadcrumbs={[
            { label: "Home", href: "/" },
            { label: "Blog", href: "/blog" },
            { label: "Delhi Survival", href: "/blog/delhi-survival-guide" },
            { label: "Avoid Getting Overcharged in Delhi" },
        ]}
        gallery={[auto, shop, upi]}
        cta={{
            title: "Move smarter, not harder.",
            description:
                "Delhi Concierge helps you navigate pricing, movement, and decisions with clarity.",
            buttonText: "Explore Delhi Guides",
            link: "/blog",
        }}
        finalThought={{
            title: "Final Thought",
            unbiased_opinion:
                "In Delhi, prices aren’t confusing — they’re conversational. Once you understand that, you’re already ahead.",
        }}
        date="March 24, 2026"
        author="Shaw"
        readTimeInMinutes={5}
        nextBlog={{
            title1: "Delhi Street Food Guide",
            description1:
                "What to eat, where to eat, and how to enjoy street food safely.",
            link1: "/blog/delhi-street-food-guide",
            image1: shop,
            category1: "Food",
            title2: "Is Delhi Safe at Night? What Actually Matters",
            description2:
                "Forget myths. Here’s what truly affects your safety in Delhi.",
            link2: "/blog/delhi-night-safety",
            image2: auto,
            category2: "Safety",
        }}
    >
        <!-- BLOG CONTENT -->
        <article class="blog-content">
            <h2>How Pricing Actually Works in Delhi</h2>

            <p>
                One of the first things people notice in Delhi is that prices
                don’t always feel fixed.
            </p>

            <p>
                You might hear different numbers for the same thing, or notice
                that the price changes depending on the situation. At first,
                this can feel confusing—but it’s not random.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                In many places, pricing in Delhi is conversational, not fixed.
            </p>

            <p>
                Especially in local markets, street shopping, or auto rides, the
                first price is often just a starting point. It’s part of an
                interaction, not a final decision.
            </p>

            <p>
                Locals understand this instinctively. They don’t react to the
                first number—they respond to it. And over time, this becomes
                completely natural.
            </p>

            <h2>Bargaining Is Part of the Experience</h2>

            <p>
                Bargaining in Delhi isn’t uncomfortable or aggressive—it’s
                normal.
            </p>

            <p>
                In markets like Palika Bazaar, Sarojini Nagar, or Nehru Place,
                negotiation is expected. It’s how buying and selling naturally
                happens.
            </p>

            <p>A typical flow looks like this:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>the seller quotes a higher price</li>
                <li>the buyer responds with a lower one (often around half)</li>
                <li>both sides gradually move toward a middle ground</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                It’s less about getting the lowest price, and more about
                reaching a price both sides are comfortable with.
            </p>

            <p>
                What surprises most people is how relaxed this process is.
                There’s often humor, light conversation, and sometimes even no
                intention to buy at all.
            </p>

            <p>
                Yes—people bargain just for the experience. And vendors are
                completely used to it.
            </p>

            <p>
                At the same time, not everything needs to be negotiated. If
                something is already reasonably priced, many locals simply pay
                and move on.
            </p>

            <h2>A Small Shift That Changes Everything</h2>

            <p>
                The moment you understand that pricing is flexible in some
                places and fixed in others, the confusion starts to fade.
            </p>

            <p>
                You stop wondering, “Am I being overcharged?” and start
                thinking, “Okay, what’s the right way to approach this?”
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                That shift—from doubt to awareness—is what makes navigating
                Delhi feel easier.
            </p>

            <p>And once you have that clarity, the rest becomes simple.</p>

            <h2>Two Rules That Make Everything Easier</h2>

            <p>
                There are a lot of small situations in Delhi where pricing can
                feel unclear. But if you follow just two simple habits, most of
                that confusion disappears on its own.
            </p>

            <h3>1. Don’t accept “free gifts”</h3>

            <p>
                At some point, someone will offer you something extra—a small
                item, an add-on, a “gift.” It feels friendly in the moment, and
                often it is.
            </p>

            <p>
                But what usually happens next is subtle. The total price shifts.
                Not always in an obvious way, and not always with bad intent—it
                just gets adjusted somewhere in the final amount.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                If you didn’t ask for it, you don’t need it.
            </p>

            <p>
                A simple “no, it’s okay” keeps everything clean. No confusion,
                no awkwardness, no recalculations later.
            </p>

            <h3>2. Agree on the price first</h3>

            <p>This is the single most useful habit you can build.</p>

            <p>Before you:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>get into a rickshaw</li>
                <li>accept a service</li>
                <li>or decide to buy something</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Just pause and ask:</p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">“Kitna hoga?” (How much will it be?)</p>

            <p>
                That one question removes uncertainty. Once both sides are
                aligned at the start, everything that follows feels smoother and
                more relaxed.
            </p>

            <p>You’re not negotiating—you’re just creating clarity.</p>

            <h2>Handling Extra Charges (Without Stress)</h2>

            <p>
                Even when you do everything right, there may be moments where
                something extra is asked at the end.
            </p>

            <p>
                It might be a small amount, or a vague explanation that wasn’t
                mentioned earlier. This is where most people hesitate—not
                because it’s serious, but because it’s unexpected.
            </p>

            <p>The easiest way to handle it is to stay calm and simple.</p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                If it wasn’t agreed before, you can say no.
            </p>

            <p>
                A clear “Nahi, itna hi tha” is usually enough. No need to
                explain or justify. Most situations resolve immediately.
            </p>

            <h2>What Most People Get Wrong</h2>

            <p>
                Many first-time visitors feel like they need to be constantly
                alert or overly sharp in every interaction.
            </p>

            <p>But that’s not how locals move through the city.</p>

            <p>Most people aren’t trying to win every deal. They’re simply:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>aware of the situation</li>
                <li>clear about basic expectations</li>
                <li>and relaxed in how they respond</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                Confidence here comes from understanding, not control.
            </p>

            <p>Once you shift into that mindset, everything feels lighter.</p>

            <h2>A Simple Safety Backup</h2>

            <p>
                In rare situations where something genuinely feels off—not just
                confusing, but uncomfortable—it helps to know there’s support
                available.
            </p>

            <p>Delhi has a dedicated Tourist Police helpline:</p>

            <p><strong>+91 8750871111</strong></p>

            <p>
                They’re experienced in assisting travelers and can step in if
                needed. You’ll likely never need it—but knowing it exists adds a
                layer of confidence.
            </p>

            <h2>The Real Shift</h2>

            <p>
                At the beginning, every small interaction can feel like a
                decision.
            </p>

            <p>“Is this fair?” “Should I question this?” “Am I overpaying?”</p>

            <p>That constant thinking is what creates the discomfort.</p>

            <p>But after a few days, something changes.</p>

            <ul>
                <li>you start recognizing fair prices</li>
                <li>you respond without hesitation</li>
                <li>you feel more at ease in conversations</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                What felt uncertain starts to feel familiar.
            </p>

            <h2>The Real Insight</h2>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                In Delhi, pricing isn’t random—it’s situational. Once you
                understand the pattern, the uncertainty disappears.
            </p>

            <p>
                You don’t need to be perfect at bargaining or hyper-aware all
                the time.
            </p>

            <p>Just a few small habits:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>checking a reference price</li>
                <li>confirming before starting</li>
                <li>staying calm in conversations</li>
            </ul>

            <p>And suddenly, you’re not reacting anymore—you’re navigating.</p>

            <p>
                That’s when Delhi starts to feel less overwhelming and more
                intuitive.
            </p>

            <FAQ faqs={faqs} />
        </article>

        <!-- SAME CSS (reuse your existing) -->
        <style>
            /* ========================= */
            /* SIGNATURE HIGHLIGHT BLOCK */
            /* ========================= */

            .highlight-bar {
                position: relative;
                padding-left: 14px;
                margin: 14px 0;
                font-weight: 500;
                color: var(--text-primary);
            }

            .highlight-bar::before {
                content: "";
                position: absolute;
                left: 0;
                top: 4px;
                width: 4px;
                height: calc(100% - 8px);
                border-radius: 2px;
                background: linear-gradient(180deg, #ff8c40, #ff6a2a);
            }
            .blog-content {
                font-size: 16px;
                line-height: 1.65;
                color: var(--text-primary);
            }

            .blog-content > * + * {
                margin-top: 10px;
            }

            .blog-content h2 {
                font-size: 22px;
                font-weight: 600;
                margin-top: 18px;
            }

            .blog-content h3 {
                font-size: 17px;
                font-weight: 600;
                margin-top: 12px;
            }

            .blog-content p {
                margin: 0;
                color: var(--text-secondary);
            }
        </style>
    </BlogTemplate>

    <CTA
        title="Navigate Delhi without second guessing."
        text="We help you understand how the city actually works—so you don’t have to figure it out alone."
        buttonText="Explore Services"
        buttonLink="/services"
    />
</BaseLayout>
